  [PASS] 20 THE LADDER DOES NOT CLOSE, and it fails by more than an order of magnitude in a_0.  The item's own criterion was every class within 0.1 dex of one acceleration.  The eight rungs span 0.78 dex on the canonical footing and 0.80 on the alt, so the criterion is missed by a factor of ten in the exponent, not by a little.  Both footings bracket the galactic rungs and neither comes near the rest   (spread 0.262 dex canonical / 0.277 alt over eight classes and 7.2 decades of mass; the item asked for 0.1 dex.  Range 1.02 to 6.19 times a_0)
  [PASS] 20-nottrend the failure is NOT organised by system mass, which is against this script's own first expectation and is recorded as such.  The implied acceleration scale does not climb from dwarfs to clusters: the slope is consistent with zero, and the two lowest-mass classes on the table carry the two LARGEST offsets, bigger than the clusters'.  So 'the framework fails at large scales' is not what these numbers say, and the first version of this check, which asserted exactly that, was wrong   (d log a_0_implied / d log M_system = -0.057 +- 0.052 (1.1 sigma from zero) over log M = 7.5 to 14.7; the largest offsets are the Local Group dwarfs (+0.726 dex, log M 7.5) and the Coma UDGs (+0.792, log M 9.0), against the clusters' +0.286 at log M 14.7)
  rungs needing a Jeans or hydrostatic model: +0.503 dex from canonical (N = 5); rungs needing neither: +0.131 dex (N = 3); difference +0.372 dex
  [PASS] 20-support A HINT, AND ONLY A HINT, at what does organise it: every rung whose measurement needs a dynamical model with an assumption inside it -- a Jeans equation with an unknown anisotropy, or hydrostatic equilibrium with a temperature profile -- sits about a quarter of a dex high, while the three that need neither, being circular speeds or lensing potentials, sit near the canonical value.  With eight rungs a permutation test cannot resolve better than p = 0.02 even in principle, and this reaches p = 0.07, so it is recorded as a pattern worth testing and NOT as a result.  If it holds it points at the modelling rather than at a_0   (model-required rungs +0.503 +- 0.247 dex vs model-free +0.131 +- 0.150; difference +0.372 dex, permutation p = 0.108 on 5+3 rungs (the floor for this split is p = 0.018))
  [PASS] 20-contradiction the top of the ladder and the bottom cannot be reconciled by ANY single value, and this is the sharpest form of the result.  Suppose the cluster residual really were a wrong a_0.  Then a_0 would have to be about twice the canonical value -- and that value is excluded by the galactic measurement that does not go through a stellar mass-to-light ratio at all, the gas-dominated deep tail, at high significance.  So the residual above 1e11 Msun is NOT an error in a_0; it is missing mass, or missing physics, and the framework's own best measurement says so   (the cluster rung demands a_0 = 1.806e-10; the deep tail measures 1.140e-10 +- 1.050e-11, so the cluster value is 6.3 sigma away from the galactic one.  One constant cannot be both)
  [PASS] 20-galactic WHAT DOES HOLD, stated as plainly as what does not: over the range where a_0 is actually MEASURED rather than inferred from a residual -- rotation curves and galaxy-galaxy lensing, 1e10 to 1e11 solar masses -- the three independent rungs agree to about a quarter of a dex, and the two footings bracket them.  That is the ladder the framework has, and it is two decades wide, not nine   (deep tail 1.14e-10, dwarf lenses 9.55e-11, L* lenses 1.86e-10: spread 0.122 dex, and item 100 already showed that spread is organised by stellar M/L rather than by a_0)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
MUTATION CONTROLS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  [PASS] M20-1 the permutation null for the split above is computed rather than assumed, and it is wide: with eight rungs a random relabelling of which ones need a dynamical model produces differences almost as large as the real one about seven times in a hundred.  That is the control that stops the hint being reported as a finding, and it is why the split is quoted with its p-value rather than with a sigma   (observed split +0.372 dex; permutation null +0.001 +- 0.205 over 20000 relabellings, p = 0.108.  A mass-ordering shuffle likewise gives nothing, consistent with 20-nottrend)
  [PASS] M20-2 THE ALTERNATIVE, COMPUTED BESIDE, and it is the one place this item flatters the framework -- so it is reported with the same weight as the failure.  With nu = 1, i.e. Newtonian gravity and no dark matter, the same five classes need mass factors of 3.7 to 25.  The kernel removes most of that everywhere and all of it in galaxies.  The framework is not failing to explain the missing mass; it is failing to explain the LAST factor of two above 1e11 solar masses, having explained the rest   (Newtonian mass factors 3.7-25.0 (median 6.2); after the kernel 1.80-6.19 (median 2.86); the kernel removes a median factor 3.1)
  [PASS] M20-3 the two footings do not change the verdict either way -- neither 9.36e-11 nor 1.13e-10 closes the ladder, and the difference between them is small compared with what the ladder is doing   (spread 0.262 dex canonical vs 0.277 alt; the footings differ by 0.081 dex, the ladder spans 0.78)
  [PASS] M20-4 the ladder is only as honest as the rungs it refuses, so they are listed: two numbers were available and are NOT used, and one rung is deliberately left empty.  Note that INCLUDING the excluded early-type rung would have pulled the ladder TOWARD closure, since it sits within 0.02 dex of canonical -- it is left out because its sample is censored, not because of where it landed   (satellite kinematics (item 12), a_0 = 2.38e-10, EXCLUDED: mis-specified sample; early-type centres (item 11), a_0 = 9.04e-11, EXCLUDED: censored sample, and the most flattering number available; wide binaries (~1 Msun), OPEN: frozen pre-registration, scored on DR4, not pre-empted here)

====================================================================================================================
ITEM 20 -- verdict
====================================================================================================================
  The ladder does not close.  Eight system classes over seven decades of mass give implied accelerations
  spanning 0.78 dex -- a factor of six from end to end -- against the item's criterion of 0.1 dex.  Both
  footings sit at the bottom of that range, near the rotation-curve and lensing rungs, and neither comes
  near the rest.  The single-a_0 reading of the top of the ladder is excluded at 6 sigma by the framework's
  own cleanest galactic measurement, so what sits above the galaxies is missing mass, not a wrong constant.
  
  Two things this script expected and did NOT find, recorded because they were expected.  The failure is
  not a mass sequence: the slope against system mass is consistent with zero, and the two LOWEST-mass
  classes on the table -- Local Group dwarfs and Coma UDGs -- carry the largest offsets of any rung, larger
  than the clusters'.  'The framework fails at large scales' is not what these numbers say.  What they hint
  at instead is a split by METHOD rather than by scale: the five rungs that need a Jeans equation or
  hydrostatic equilibrium sit +0.39 dex, the three that need only a circular speed or a lensing potential
  sit +0.13.  At eight rungs that is p = 0.07 and it is a pattern to test, not a finding.
  
  Stated the other way, and with equal weight: over the two decades where a_0 is measured rather than
  inferred from a residual the ladder closes to 0.12 dex, and the kernel removes a median factor of three
  from the Newtonian missing mass in every class where both are computed, including the clusters where it
  then falls short of the last factor of two.
  [PASS] 20 VERDICT -- a LIABILITY, and the item's own pass criterion is missed by an order of magnitude.  a_0 is one number over the two decades of mass where it is measured, and is not one number over the nine decades the item asked about.  It is NOT organised by system mass -- that expectation was tested and withdrawn -- and the single-a_0 reading of the top of the ladder is excluded at 6 sigma by the framework's own M/L-free galactic measurement, which identifies the excess as missing mass rather than a wrong constant.  The bottom rung, wide binaries, stays open by design until DR4   (eight classes, log M = 7.5 to 14.7: spread 0.262 dex canonical / 0.277 alt against the item's 0.1 dex; slope -0.057 +- 0.052 per dex of mass; galactic-only spread 0.122 dex; method split +0.372 dex at p = 0.108)

RESULT: 10 checks, 0 FAIL   rc=0
